{"data": {"id": 160092, "accession_number": "1997.60", "share_license_status": "CC0", "tombstone": "Deer Effigy Vessel, 250–600 CE. Mesoamerica, Maya style, Classic Period (250-900). Earthenware; overall: 22.8 x 24.7 x 18 cm (9 x 9 3/4 x 7 1/16 in.). The Cleveland Museum of Art, Leonard C. Hanna Jr. Fund, 1997.60", "current_location": "233 Mesoamerican and Intermediate Region", "title": "Deer Effigy Vessel", "creation_date": "250–600 CE", "creation_date_earliest": 250, "creation_date_latest": 600, "artists_tags": [], "culture": ["Mesoamerica, Maya style, Classic Period (250-900)"], "technique": "earthenware", "support_materials": [], "department": "Art of the Americas", "collection": "AA - Mesoamerica", "type": "Ceramic", "measurements": "Overall: 22.8 x 24.7 x 18 cm (9 x 9 3/4 x 7 1/16 in.)", "dimensions": {"overall": {"height": 0.228, "height_inch": 9, "height_inch_fraction": 0.0, "width": 0.247, "width_inch": 9, "width_inch_fraction": 0.75, "depth": 0.18, "depth_inch": 7, "depth_inch_fraction": 0.0625}}, "state_of_the_work": null, "edition_of_the_work": null, "copyright": null, "inscriptions": [], "exhibitions": {"current": [], "legacy": []}, "provenance": [], "find_spot": null, "related_works": [], "former_accession_numbers": [], "did_you_know": null, "early_education_description": null, "artlens_description": null, "description": "Maya artwork from the Early Classic Period (about AD 250-600) is dominated by three-dimensional sculpture in jade, stone, wood, and especially ceramic. This vessel is an exceptionally elegant example of Maya blackware, produced by firing earthenware pottery in a reducing (low oxygen) atmosphere. The deer is frequently depicted in Maya art, usually as a victim of either hunting or sacrifice. Venus signs and conch shells engraved on the deer also suggest death, for the planet Venus was regarded as a bringer of ill-fortune, and conch shell trumpets were carried by hunters. The small frog or toad pressed firmly under the deer's hoof relates to agricultural fertility. The croaking of these amphibians was a harbinger of rain.", "external_resources": {"wikidata": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60745933"], "internet_archive": ["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-1997.60-deer-effigy-vessel"]}, "citations": [{"citation": "Cleveland Museum of Art, “Museum Acquires Major Chuck Close Painting, 19th-century Fire Screen, Rare Prints &amp; Drawings,” June 9, 1997, Cleveland Museum of Art Archives.", "page_number": null, "url": "https://archive.org/details/cmapr4136"}, {"citation": "Young- Sanchez, Margaret, <em>Cleveland Art: The Cleveland Museum of Art Members Magazine</em>. Vol. 37 no. 09, November 1997", "page_number": "Mentioned & reproduced: cover, 4-5", "url": "https://archive.org/details/CMAMM1997-09/page/4"}], "url": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1997.60", "images": {"annotation": null, "web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_web.jpg", "width": "1050", "height": "893", "filesize": "71386", "filename": "1997.60_web.jpg"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "2892", "filesize": "794926", "filename": "1997.60_print.jpg"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_full.tif", "width": "4838", "height": "4116", "filesize": "59771380", "filename": "1997.60_full.tif"}}, "alternate_images": [{"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_alt0_web.jpg", "width": "1056", "height": "893", "filesize": "74523"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_alt0_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "2876", "filesize": "809403"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_alt0_full.tif", "width": "4911", "height": "4155", "filesize": "61246148"}, "date_created": "2011-12-14T18:46:56", "annotation": ""}, {"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1997.60/1997.60_alt1_web.jpg"